## Language

Always reply in 繁體中文, including after a long session or a compaction, and when relaying a subagent's English report. Code, identifiers and technical terms stay in their original form.

{{ if eq .agent "codex" -}}
## Skills

Use the shared skills in `~/.agents/skills`. The skill-patches plugin injects patches on explicit skill mentions, observed skill reads and matching MCP calls. When a skill's patch has not already been supplied, read its matching `<name>.patch/SKILL.md`, when present, from `~/.claude/skills`, `~/.agents/skills`, then the repository root's `.claude/skills` and `.agents/skills`; deduplicate symlinked files and apply patches in that order. Project patches come last and override global ones. A patch extends its base skill; invoke the base workflow.

For a project workflow absent from the skill catalog, check `.claude/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and `.claude/commands/<name>.md` and follow the matching instructions with available Codex tools.

## Claude instructions

Codex reads `CLAUDE.md` as a fallback when a directory has no `AGENTS.md`. If both exist, follow `AGENTS.md` and read `CLAUDE.md` when it contains additional project guidance. Resolve Claude tool names to available Codex equivalents; use shell commands for file reads and checks, skills for workflows, and supported subagent tools for explicitly delegated work.

When a `CLAUDE.md` uses `@path` imports, read the referenced instructions relative to that file (or expand `~/` against the user's home), once per file to avoid import cycles.

{{ end -}}
## Code search

Route by what the question asks for:

| Ask | Tool |
|---|---|
| **Intent** — where is X implemented, how does Y work, code like this spot | `mcp__semble__search` (`content`: `docs`\|`config`\|`all` beyond code; `repo`: the project root); from a hit, `mcp__semble__find_related` |
| **Literal** — every occurrence of a string or regex, all callers of a symbol, an exact message | `rg`{{ if eq .agent "claude" }} (the Grep tool is ripgrep). Large repo (the search-tools plugin runs `tgrep serve` for it): `tgrep` from `<root>` with the same flags{{ end }} |
| **Shape** — a construct with variable parts (`foo($$$ARGS)`), a mechanical rewrite across files, a file's symbol outline | `ast-grep run -p '<pattern>' -l <lang>`; `-r '<fix>'` shows a diff, `-U` applies it; `ast-grep outline <path>` |

One search per question: land on the top intent hit and read there; the exhaustive list is for literal asks. From Bash, a tree search is `rg -n <pattern> [path]`{{ if eq .agent "claude" }} (`grep -r` and `xargs grep` are refused by the plugin and cost a retry).

Before the first `tgrep` or `ast-grep` call in a session, load the `search-tools:reference` skill.{{ else }}. If the Semble MCP is unavailable, use `semble search` with the same query and content selection.{{ end }}

## File references

{{ if eq .agent "codex" -}}
Link every local file or directory to its absolute path using Markdown, with an optional `:line` suffix, for example `[src/app.ts:42](/absolute/path/src/app.ts:42)`. Use forward slashes; on Windows include the drive letter, as in `C:/Users/...`. Code blocks and commands keep plain paths.
{{ else -}}
Write every file or directory you mention in a reply (a worktree too) as a markdown link to its absolute `file://` URL, with the repo-relative path (and `:line` when it points at a line) as the text: [src/app.ts:42](file:///home/me/repo/src/app.ts). The terminal turns it into a clickable OSC 8 link; a bare path is not clickable. Forward slashes, spaces as `%20`{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "windows" }}, drive letter after the third slash (`file:///C:/Users/...`){{ end }}. Code blocks and commands keep plain paths.
{{ end }}
A web address gets the same shape, with a short name as the text, never a bare URL. Every pull request, merge request, issue, pipeline or commit you mention is such a link, named by its short form: [herdr#4748](https://github.com/herdrdev/herdr/issues/4748), [CRM!2612](https://git.gss.com.tw/CRM/CRM/-/merge_requests/2612); a bare `#4748` or `!2612` is not clickable.

## Dotfiles

chezmoi manages `~/.claude`, the shared Codex instructions and settings sync helper under `~/.codex`, `~/.gitconfig` and the shared `~/.config` entries; `chezmoi managed` lists them, `~/.local/share/chezmoi/README.md` maps the source. Change a target only through its source (`chezmoi edit <target>`, then `chezmoi apply`): a drifted target makes `chezmoi apply` stop on an interactive prompt and hang the shell. In `settings.json`, only the keys listed in `.chezmoidata/claude.toml` come from the source; every other key stays local. Codex config stays local too: `~/.codex/sync-settings.py` merges the repo-owned keys and MCP connection fields while preserving other settings and comments.

When `chezmoi status` shows drift, run `chezmoi diff <target>`, then `chezmoi apply --force <target>`. An `R` line is a script that runs on every apply, not drift.

## Commands for the user

Before writing a command for the user to run, decide where it runs and write it in that shell's syntax, the fence language naming it:

{{ if eq .chezmoi.os "windows" -}}
{{ if eq .agent "claude" -}}
- A `!` command runs in the Bash tool's Git Bash: forward slashes, no PowerShell cmdlets, fenced as `bash`.
{{ else -}}
- Commands executed by Codex use the active session shell; inspect it before choosing PowerShell or Bash syntax.
{{ end -}}
- A command for the user's own terminal runs in PowerShell 7 (pwsh), fenced as `powershell`.
{{- else -}}
- A `!` command and a command for the user's own terminal both run in zsh, fenced as `zsh`.
{{- end }}
- The user's message overrides that default: a named shell, a pasted prompt (`PS C:\>`, `C:\>`, `$`, `%`), or an error from one. Write cmd syntax only then.
- `!` commands get no stdin: a login that asks to paste a key or answer a prompt is a terminal command, for a separate window.

## Changes

- Verify a tool's setting or capability in its docs or source ({{ if eq .agent "claude" }}the `researcher` agent, {{ end }}the `find-docs` skill) before stating or configuring it; a claim you could not check says "unverified".
- Before editing a config file chezmoi does not manage, copy it to `<file>.bak-<yyyymmdd>`, and list every touched file in the reply, so a revert is one copy back.
- A visual choice (glyphs, colors, layout): show the options for the user to preview (`html_preview`, or an HTML file to open) and edit once they pick one.
- Stage commits by path (`git add <path>...`): the guard hook rejects whole-tree staging, and each rejection costs a retry.
- A merge, a close, or a pick between open options waits for the user's answer to that item; "繼續" or "continue" moves the rest of the work forward around it, and the reply lists what still waits.
- A pull request merges only after the user's go-ahead (above) and, where the repository has a pipeline, only on a green one. When a configured pipeline does not start (billing, quota), say so and leave the merge waiting; a local check does not stand in for it.
- A file committed to a repository names no path that exists only on this machine (a drive letter, a user folder, a local folder's name); home-relative and repository-relative paths are fine. A machine-local value lives in the repository's own local-override mechanism (a gitignored `.local/`, a `*.local.*` file, or local git config), not in a new environment variable, and the committed file names only where to find it.
- Work a program can decide that you have run by hand twice this session (a merge-and-clean chain, a setup, a cleanup) becomes a script (py, js, ps1 or sh) in the repository you are working in; name it in the reply, and add a package when the standard library falls short.
- A brief marks each fact about state (a game, CI, a device, a cause) `verified` (checked this session) or `unverified`.
{{ if eq .agent "claude" -}}
- Delegate with the model matched to the task: `haiku` for mechanical inventories, `sonnet` by default, `opus` for hard reasoning. Pass `effort` with it: `low` for mechanical inventories, `high` for bug hunts and hard reasoning, `medium` otherwise. A named agent's frontmatter already sets both: keep its model and effort, and raise it to `opus` with `high` only to retry a diagnosis its `sonnet` run got wrong.
- A brief names no model for commit trailers: the subagent copies its own attribution reminder.
{{ else -}}
- When delegating work, use the available Codex model and agent catalog; inherit the session model unless the user or applicable workflow selects another. Claude model aliases and agent frontmatter need a supported Codex equivalent.
{{ end }}

## Reports

- A question for the user defines each term they have not seen, with the real screen or output beside it when there is one, and says where every number comes from. A relayed subagent report carries what the user must decide.
- A change that shows on screen (a UI, a bot run, a live test) comes back with evidence: a clip or annotated stills (the `screen-record` and `evidence-annotate` skills), as finished as the run allows.

## T3 Code

When the `t3-code` MCP tools are present, the session runs in a T3 thread:

- Long or live delegated work runs through the `t3-code` `delegate_task`, so it shows in the user's sidebar; a native subagent is for a short read whose result comes straight back.
- `link_pull_request` is best effort: on an error, say so in one line and carry on with the task in the same turn.

## Tools

- Package manager: pnpm, unless the project's own lockfile names another. A global CLI from the npm registry installs with `pnpm add -g`.
- Wire an external tool in as its CLI plus a skill, not an MCP server: the CLI goes in `.chezmoidata/tools.toml`, the skill in `.chezmoidata/skills.toml`. An MCP server is for a tool that must stay resident (semble's index); it goes in `[claude.mcp]` of `.chezmoidata/claude.toml`.
- Declare native agent plugins and marketplaces in `.chezmoidata/plugins.toml`; `~/.agents/sync-plugins.py` reconciles them through each host's CLI. Existing local disabled choices stay local; update explicitly with `--update`.
{{ if eq .agent "claude" -}}
- Wait on a long job by starting it with `run_in_background`, then end the turn: its completion notice resumes you. Poll only state that sends no notice (a CI pipeline, a remote deploy).
- A notice that a background task is still waiting on its own work ends the turn with no text; a finish, a failure, or a question for the user gets one reply.
- Edit one place with the Edit tool, which refuses an ambiguous match.{{ else -}}
- Wait on long jobs using the session or task identifier returned by the execution tool, and report completion after checking its exit status.
- Make focused edits with the available patch tool.{{ end }} A scripted batch of edits asserts that each search string occurs exactly once and passes each replacement literally: a function in JavaScript (`s.replace(a, () => b)`), so a `$` in the new text stays literal; Python's `str.replace` is literal already, `re.sub` takes a lambda.
{{- if ne .chezmoi.os "windows" }}
- The shell is zsh with `noclobber`: overwrite an existing file with `>|`, since a plain `>` onto it fails with "file exists".
- Stop processes by PID: `pgrep -f <pattern>`, check the list, then `kill <pid>`. `pkill -f` also matches the shell running it whenever the pattern sits in that shell's own command line, and kills it; a `pgrep -f` wait loop matches itself the same way and never ends.
{{- end }}
{{- if eq .chezmoi.os "windows" }}

## Windows shell

- Before a Bash command that names a `/tmp` path another program reads (node, python, pwsh), passes a path or another program's output between Git Bash, Node and pwsh, feeds a script to an interpreter (`-e`, `-c`, a pipe or heredoc), holds a `\\`, or runs `python -I` / `-E` on text with CJK, read `~/.claude/docs/windows-shell.md`: path rewriting, two `/tmp` folders, `\r` line ends, console encoding, pwsh arguments, doubled backslashes, inline scripts.
{{- end }}
